#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3263b66-c96c-554b-8cc8-e566d2055a9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/dc6d39b05ae4862121b7b76b555be7b19931eda478e84973181e74e8d628db2b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289291} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a9d4c9-9f57-5de7-9c26-45f26af76718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/130f215103a0c955d14ac386a382afae09c22f507491bc54a408ad13a154cbc5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289292} {:answer/id #uuid "86e382dc-1902-5596-8db6-d162368c6dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/e1faae8b1750b1c402f88ef801cd6609d259c37c523ac4bf4a7cb795034a9bd1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289293} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2741bf-cc00-5eed-9af1-f8df6297c68c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/f5be8864e70a021eb2fa717a9a7998c645e3792a68d23de54a07114b7a566cf1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289294} {:answer/id #uuid "7c484290-5833-5e20-b12f-a74ffd539d46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/6b0143a0adc22d1834fc404c9da0d03b2143c8cbc16a29a9286637920694002d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86e382dc-1902-5596-8db6-d162368c6dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/e1faae8b1750b1c402f88ef801cd6609d259c37c523ac4bf4a7cb795034a9bd1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289293} :answer-field/id #uuid "775e0b0b-3551-5937-ab83-17e087f1afa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "708bc623-27d8-59ae-aefa-3003b8b7f233" :question/math-academy-id "q-147523" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/771f1ffeda2b0041e1485611315661eed0bc88a601513d4fd4b2088022521aa8.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,2)$, $(1,-1)$,  and $(-2,2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/e1faae8b1750b1c402f88ef801cd6609d259c37c523ac4bf4a7cb795034a9bd1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2477336-349a-5aac-93c2-f4350e74228a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289298} {:answer/id #uuid "8d64c833-fc9e-509d-83dc-ff3ea3ebafdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289299} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0da92f-535a-53fe-90a8-68a6ef0418d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289300} {:answer/id #uuid "743efd37-2195-587f-b67e-a6b6f044f636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289301} {:answer/id #uuid "b82d7844-22fd-5bff-a00d-3fd36016ed69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff0da92f-535a-53fe-90a8-68a6ef0418d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289300} :answer-field/id #uuid "e33b8c87-45db-5a5f-88c5-186cadf6ac06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb71ccfe-145d-5547-982d-cefa0d8df4a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-147670" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,1),(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$  \n\n- $(6,-18)$\n- $(-5,-20)$\n- $(12,-24)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -24 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are  $(12,-24),(0,0)$ and $(-6,12).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b673724a-4915-517c-b9a0-fe79b8e7faa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289305} {:answer/id #uuid "aec1f854-7d74-50f2-a59b-e2ff15f06aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289306} {:answer/id #uuid "7b50d5f9-72ad-547c-a124-76efa4e14d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289307} {:answer/id #uuid "c1bf9be5-b982-5166-9542-007bcdd73298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289308} {:answer/id #uuid "24847843-649b-595c-82b8-e8e87f70614a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1bf9be5-b982-5166-9542-007bcdd73298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289308} :answer-field/id #uuid "e42fdd2c-3928-5581-8f96-17bc27665aed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5cfe0cab-3f64-5782-9a2c-dd556be2b484" :question/math-academy-id "q-162202" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/8978d3bb02b4032b6e3a8b4a85878db647eb8323ac5b0423b5a9a6fab121c7e7.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-8)-4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca92b4c-bc03-577b-8a42-11e52981c0a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289312} {:answer/id #uuid "0e950484-dbc4-522d-81c1-37497abf293f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289313} {:answer/id #uuid "fff3f9d7-1b1b-5880-b370-424998a56ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289314} {:answer/id #uuid "62aaa7c9-3ff7-51da-b2b9-a1e2d05ccad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289315} {:answer/id #uuid "549ca8fa-9331-5ead-a01a-20addbd7da9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62aaa7c9-3ff7-51da-b2b9-a1e2d05ccad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289315} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7ad26f2-6912-513b-b733-e76e602210c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bea7908-25f3-50cf-9a41-019383192d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-162208" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/0231cc5aaf661cc873f18ff676a03d66927be48b49d38649efc45860197d18c1.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "577bd934-421d-54ec-b0b2-d003700ec403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/461ff9fb5ef66239eb81314f761fac9ceb32b3bc88df6940b61445c752fe3d0c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289319} {:answer/id #uuid "700107e3-638e-5589-9221-901c6ac2b7d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/bf7cc4e728cd1ade741c55b2fbb54720220e0e09a8521a150300532b4e3ee9ed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289320} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb774ed-95c1-54d0-a9ab-0cbb1b865b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/ed8a2354f8cc04f998875f599a52b50e6c64cafe8976f1bea176772543e295ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289321} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9411e7-77cf-54e3-a181-f1015dfa57ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/0af059340bdef0548876baad9bcdf990f6bc92330617dde33c18c00578aecad1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289322} {:answer/id #uuid "eb1006cf-3407-53b0-b4de-08d098a9bd1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/da61a7fd99ce683be4e66d3708ddf15c969b038f0250dc4c024ace870c3f7544.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf9411e7-77cf-54e3-a181-f1015dfa57ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/0af059340bdef0548876baad9bcdf990f6bc92330617dde33c18c00578aecad1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289322} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c0ee37d-c540-532c-b043-87ace79e55ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f28ce8d4-0044-5e40-a90c-5299f41f2c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-21065" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/db7d2b33f2badad28b754d4482047a33bb37469ed8b7b17859b10552840e2f51.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,4)$, \n$(-6,12)$ and $(-1,2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/2e1dde461c6244a59672fc28c842b70a30297ac7a8c7e7cf2095a973cd1860cd.png)"}]}
